# Forge's private environment (copy it to config/forge.env, which is gitignored). ./forge loads it for you.
# Never commit the real file. Move secrets and state between machines through a secure channel outside the repo.

# The target project's repo root (example-project by default). Leaving it empty finds the sibling directory
# ../example-project automatically, so if both live under the same workspace/ there is nothing to fill in.
FORGE_PROJECT_ROOT=

# The webhook for result cards (the "custom bot" webhook URL of your dogfood chat).
# Left empty, results are only written back as comments on the PRD document through feishu-doc.js, with no
# card posted to the chat.
FEISHU_REVIEW_WEBHOOK=
# Optional: the webhook signing secret, when the chat bot has signature verification switched on.
FEISHU_REVIEW_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The dogfood chat's chat_id (used by the polling entry point; it can stay empty if you only ever `forge add`
# by hand).
FEISHU_REVIEW_CHAT_ID=

# -- Bot direct messages (awaiting confirmation, awaiting go, failures and completions are pushed to your DM,
# and the button callbacks come back the same way) --
# Reusing the lark-mcp app means granting it im:message:send in the Feishu admin console first (the link is in
# deploy/README.md). Left empty, it degrades to the webhook and local desktop notifications.
FEISHU_BOT_APP_ID=
FEISHU_BOT_APP_SECRET=
# The bot's own open_id, used by the channel intake gate to decide whether a message mentioned this bot. A
# channel message with no mention does not enter the flow, which is what stops a casually shared document
# being swallowed as a PRD.
# Left empty it is fetched from bot/v3/info automatically (which needs the bot online); fill it in to save
# that request, or to make it deterministic (from the admin console's credentials page, or bot/v3/info).
FEISHU_BOT_OPEN_ID=
FEISHU_DM_OPEN_ID=
# Or the direct-message chat id instead (open_id wins if both are set).
FEISHU_DM_CHAT_ID=
# Set to 0 to turn off the macOS desktop-notification fallback.
NOTIFY_DESKTOP=

# -- Choosing the IM provider (see src/messaging/index.ts) --
# feishu (the default -- leave it empty) or slack. **A value it does not recognise stops the service from
# starting at all**, and never falls back silently: otherwise one mistyped letter would send every approval
# card to Feishu while Slack stayed empty, with no symptom whatsoever.
FORGE_MESSAGING_PROVIDER=

# -- Slack (only needed when FORGE_MESSAGING_PROVIDER=slack) --
# At api.slack.com/apps, create an App:
#   OAuth & Permissions -> Bot Token Scopes: chat:write, channels:history, groups:history, im:history,
#     im:write, users:read
#     (do not miss im:history: subscribing to message.im without granting it means a requirement sent by DM
#      cannot be read back during offline backfill)
#   Socket Mode -> switch it on, and under Basic Information -> App-Level Tokens create a token with
#     connections:write
#   Event Subscriptions -> subscribe to message.channels / message.groups / message.im
#   Interactivity & Shortcuts -> switch it on (the forms are modals, so this is required)
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=
# The bot's own user id (App -> Basic Information in the console, or auth.test).
# **Leaving it empty is as good as switching the bot off**: a channel message cannot be checked for a mention
# of this bot, so the core conservatively ignores every one of them.
SLACK_BOT_USER_ID=
# Where direct messages are pushed: your own user id (Slack lets a user id be used as the channel of a DM).
SLACK_DM_USER_ID=
# A comma-separated list of channel ids: the channel entry point, plus the offline backfill at startup and on
# every reconnect.
SLACK_WATCH_CHANNELS=
# Optional: an Incoming Webhook URL, as the channel-side fallback when a bot direct message is not delivered.
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Optional: override the Web API base (https://slack.com/api by default). Fill it in only when restricted
# egress has to go through a corporate proxy; the local acceptance loop
# (test/slack-live-loop.test.ts) uses it too. Getting it wrong sends every call somewhere else, so normally
# leave it empty.
#SLACK_API_BASE=

# On a local machine, claude and codex use whatever each CLI is already logged in as, so there is nothing to
# fill in here.
# Fill this in later, for an unattended Mac mini (the one-year token `claude setup-token` produces):
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# -- Liveness and health (the local status page plus the watchdog) --
# The port for the health service and status page (bound to 127.0.0.1 only). Empty means use runtime.yaml's
# health.port, which defaults to 4319.
# FORGE_HEALTH_PORT=4319

# -- Splitting the control plane from the runners (Phase 2; all-in-one by default) --
# On a RUNNER: setting this makes the process a **pure runner**, pulling jobs from a remote control plane over
# HTTP (GET <url>/jobs) and reading and writing state through it (POST <url>/store).
# Empty means all-in-one: it enumerates from the local database and talks to sqlite directly, as it does today.
# FORGE_CONTROL_URL=https://control.example/forge
# The shared secret the control plane authenticates with: the runner sends `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and
# the control-plane server checks it. Both sides have to match.
# FORGE_CONTROL_TOKEN=
#
# On the CONTROL PLANE machine: the address and port its HTTP face binds to (127.0.0.1:4320 by default).
# **A `forge listen` with FORGE_CONTROL_PORT set is the control-plane process**: one process running the
# orchestration tick, its own job loop, and serving /jobs and /store to any extra runners -- a single sqlite
# connection with no contention between processes. An extra runner runs `forge listen` on another machine with
# FORGE_CONTROL_URL pointing here, and is a pure runner that only runs the job loop. `forge control` is the
# separate variant that serves the HTTP face without running the orchestration.
# Binding to anything other than the loopback address **requires** FORGE_CONTROL_TOKEN, or it fails closed and
# refuses to start -- /store can read and write every session's state.
# The control-plane machine must **not** set FORGE_CONTROL_URL, which is the marker of a runner; set it there
# and the control-plane server fails closed and refuses to start.
# FORGE_CONTROL_HOST=127.0.0.1
# FORGE_CONTROL_PORT=4320
#
# Stopping several runners from claiming the same job (the lease):
# The RUNNER's identity, which owns the lease. Empty means host:pid. Giving each runner a distinct value reads
# better, and makes it easier to audit which one claimed what.
# FORGE_RUNNER_ID=
# The lease TTL in seconds (7200, two hours, by default): how long one runner holds a job before another may
# take it. It **has to be at least as long as the longest a single tick can spend on one job**, or a long step
# outlives its lease halfway through, another runner claims the job, and two of them run in the same worktree.
# Raise it for heavy downstream work, where a single gate C or gate D step takes a long time.
# FORGE_LEASE_TTL_SEC=7200
